---
/**
 * Beats nine and ten: the rival, and the honest cost of choosing this one.
 * STORY-SPINE-2026-08-27.md has the full map. Two beats share one section
 * because on this page they are one thought — here are the tools that look
 * like the answer, and here is what picking this one costs you.
 *
 * NOBODY IS NAMED HERE, AND NOBODY MAY BE. RC-40 scopes named competitors to
 * /compare and to the condition that a cell reports what their own pricing
 * page listed on a stated date. This section argues the CATEGORY and links to
 * the table for the names. RC-47 opened 2026-08-27 for the category claim
 * itself, on the owner's request for a comparison on the homepage.
 *
 * NO SENTENCE HERE MAY SAY WHAT ANOTHER PRODUCT CANNOT DO. That is the single
 * way this section becomes a false statement about a company, and it is a live
 * pull, because the beat wants a villain. The lede therefore describes an
 * ASSUMPTION (a menu that repeats) rather than a limitation, and the turn is
 * written as the question to go and ask them, not as an answer given for them.
 *
 * THE FOUR GIVE-UPS SHARE NO ROW WITH WhoThisIsFor. That section's four are
 * about FIT — the wrong shape of kitchen, at 30% depth, sorting readers out
 * before the film plays. These four are about CAPABILITY: the right kitchen,
 * a missing feature, read by someone who already wants the thing. Saying any
 * of the same four twice would be the failure the BuiltForKitchens comment
 * records. Each is a /compare row and may not be softened here:
 *
 *   printed labels  -> "Printed USDA nutrition labels", COMING, and the only one
 *                      of the four that is. The framing sentence says three are
 *                      not planned and one is being built, because /compare's
 *                      legend exists to keep coming from reading as no and a
 *                      heading that flattens the two undoes it.  RC-42 splits
 *                      the computed facts (ship) from the panel (does not).
 *   top up to par   -> "Buying that tops up to par", no.         RC-43.
 *   dietary         -> "Dietary characteristics", no.
 *   Spanish         -> "English and Spanish", no.
 *
 * If any of those four ships, the /compare row moves first and this follows it.
 *
 * NO PRIMARY CTA. The page opens, middles and closes on one action and this is
 * not one of the three. The quiet link out to /compare is the whole ask: a
 * reader at this beat wants the table, not a trial.
 *
 * THAT IS THE SECOND /compare LINK ON THE PAGE, and it is deliberate. Counted
 * on the built homepage: six quiet links, two of them to /compare. The other is
 * WhoThisIsFor's "buy something else" at 30% depth, which is a sorting link -
 * it is aimed at a reader who should leave. This one is at 80% depth and is
 * aimed at a reader who wants to stay and check the claim. Different job,
 * different reader, half a page apart, and nothing in npm run check can see the
 * count either way. If they ever end up adjacent, this one goes.
 * (PaperIn's note that quiet secondaries are "at their cap of two" predates the
 * comparison page and the features hub, and is stale.)
 */
const giveUps = [
	{
		title: 'Printed nutrition labels',
		body: 'The fifteen nutrients an FDA label carries are computed per recipe, from USDA profiles. The printed panel is still being built.'
	},
	{
		title: 'Buying that tops you back up to par',
		body: 'Shopping is what the jobs on the books need, minus what the shelf can be trusted for. It will not order you back up to a par level.'
	},
	{
		title: 'Dietary characteristics',
		body: 'A recipe shows the heading and says plainly that it has not been assessed. Nothing is inferred from an ingredient name.'
	},
	{
		title: 'Spanish',
		body: 'English only.'
	}
];

// The figure. Seven equal marks against nine irregular ones on scattered dates:
// the restaurant's week and the caterer's month, drawn at the same scale. Bar
// heights on the right are guest counts, which is why they are uneven and why
// three dates are empty.
import SectionHandoff from '../SectionHandoff.astro';

const restaurantWeek = ['M', 'T', 'W', 'T', 'F', 'S', 'S'];
const cateringDates = [0, 3, 0, 7, 2, 0, 9, 0, 4, 0, 0, 6];
---

<section
	id="alternatives"
	class="border-y border-hairline bg-cream"
	aria-labelledby="alternatives-heading"
>
	<div class="container-page py-section">
		<!-- FIGURE LEADS SINCE 2026-08-29. The two calendars are the best drawing on
		     the page and they sat under a paragraph with the right 40% of the
		     viewport empty beside it. At lg the lede is the left column and the
		     figure the right, so the shape is in view the moment the heading is.
		     story: docs/stories/homepage-visual-guidance.story.md -->
		<div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[5fr_7fr] lg:items-start lg:gap-16">
		<div class="max-w-3xl" data-reveal>
			<p class="eyebrow">The other tools</p>
			<h2 id="alternatives-heading" class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold">
				Most kitchen software is built for the week that repeats.
			</h2>
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-[66ch] text-lede text-ink-soft">
				Recipe costing was solved for restaurants a long time ago, and the tools that
				solved it are built on the thing a restaurant has: a menu that comes back. The
				same dishes, the same covers, the same order guide every Tuesday.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-[66ch] text-ink-soft">
				So the question to take to any of them is not whether it costs a recipe. They all
				do. It is what happens to your Saturday when the client adds forty guests on
				Thursday: whether the shopping list, the prep sheet and the pack list all move with
				them, or whether that part is still yours to redo by hand.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- Drawn, not screenshotted, for the reason LoopBand is: the claim is a
		     shape, and no crop of anybody's screen can show a shape. Plain bordered
		     boxes rather than inline vector markup, so scripts/check-dist.mjs has no
		     viewBox to fail the build over. Astro ships HTML comments to dist and
		     that checker scans them, so naming the tag here failed the build once. -->
		<figure class="lg:mt-2" data-reveal>
			<div class="border-y border-ticket-rule bg-paper px-5 py-8 sm:px-8">
				<p class="sr-only">
					Two calendars drawn at the same scale. A restaurant week is seven identical
					marks, Monday to Sunday, all the same height: one menu, served again. A
					catering month is twelve dates of which only six carry a job, and every job a
					different height, because every job is a different guest count.
				</p>

				<div class="grid gap-10 sm:grid-cols-2 sm:gap-12" aria-hidden="true">
					<!-- min-w-0 on every flex child in both rows. A flex item defaults to
					     min-width:auto and refuses to shrink below its own glyph, which put the
					     twelve date marks 9px past the viewport at 200% text on a phone.
					
					     THE DATE MARKS ARE DRAWN, NOT TYPED, for the other half of that same
					     problem. min-w-0 is exactly the rule that had been holding each cell
					     open to its own character, so with it on, a bullet glyph stopped
					     overflowing the page and started clipping inside its cell instead -
					     which the page-level overflow check cannot see. A rounded box has no
					     min-content width, so it shrinks with the column.
					
					     THE FIGURE'S GEOMETRY IS IN PIXELS, NOT REM, on purpose. Bar heights,
					     gaps and dot sizes are a drawing rather than text, and rem units made
					     them grow with the reader's font size while the container did not: at
					     200% the six job dots rendered 12px wide inside an 8.2px column. The
					     labels around the drawing are still text and still scale. -->
					<div class="min-w-0">
						<p class="font-mono text-eyebrow font-semibold tracking-wide text-ink-soft">
							A RESTAURANT WEEK
						</p>
						<div class="mt-5 flex items-end gap-[6px]" style="height: 80px">
							{
								restaurantWeek.map(() => (
									<span class="h-12 min-w-0 flex-1 border-t-2 border-ink-rule/45 bg-softgreen" />
								))
							}
						</div>
						<div class="mt-2 flex gap-[6px]">
							{
								restaurantWeek.map((day) => (
									<span class="min-w-0 flex-1 text-center font-mono text-eyebrow text-ink-soft">{day}</span>
								))
							}
						</div>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-[34ch] text-sm leading-snug text-ink-soft">
							One menu, served again. The order guide is the same one as last week.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="min-w-0">
						<p class="font-mono text-eyebrow font-semibold tracking-wide text-amber-deep">
							A CATERING MONTH
						</p>
						<div class="mt-5 flex items-end gap-[6px]" style="height: 80px">
							{
								cateringDates.map((size) => (
									<span
										class:list={[
											'min-w-0 flex-1',
											size > 0 ? 'border-t-2 border-amber bg-softamber' : 'border-b border-dashed border-ink-rule/45'
										]}
										style={`height: ${size > 0 ? size * 9 : 0}px`}
									/>
								))
							}
						</div>
						<div class="mt-2 flex gap-[6px]">
							{
								cateringDates.map((size) => (
									<span class="min-w-0 flex-1">
										<span
											class:list={[
												'mx-auto block rounded-full',
												size > 0 ? 'size-[6px] bg-amber-deep' : 'size-[4px] bg-ink-rule/45'
											]}
										/>
									</span>
								))
							}
						</div>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-[34ch] text-sm leading-snug text-ink-soft">
							A different menu on every date, and a guest count somebody else chooses.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<!-- The {' '} after </strong> and before the /compare link are load-bearing.
			     A newline at an element boundary is collapsed away by the compiler, and
			     this shipped reading "the week.That is" and "on thecomparison page" once.
			     Same failure the Hero comment records for the price line. -->
			<figcaption class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-sm text-ink-soft">
				<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">The unit is the job, not the week.</strong>{' '}
				That is the assumption everything downstream is built on, which is why it is worth
				checking before you pick a tool rather than after.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
		</div>

		<div class="mt-12 border-t border-ticket-rule pt-8" data-reveal>
			<h3 class="text-h3 font-semibold">And here is what you give up.</h3>
			<p class="mt-4 max-w-[66ch] text-ink-soft">
				CostCook is one plan with everything in it, which means there is no tier to move up
				to when something is missing. Four of the things the bigger tools list are the ones
				most likely to matter to you. Three of them are not planned. One is being built,
				and it says so.
			</p>
			<ul role="list" class="mt-7 grid gap-x-12 gap-y-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
				{
					giveUps.map((entry) => (
						<li class="border-t border-hairline pt-4">
							<p class="font-semibold">{entry.title}</p>
							<p class="mt-2 max-w-[52ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">{entry.body}</p>
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ul>
			<p class="mt-8 text-sm text-ink-soft">
				Every row, including the ones where they win, is on the{' '}
				<a href="/compare" class="btn-quiet">comparison page</a>. It names them and reports
				what their own pricing pages listed, on a date printed at the bottom.
			</p>
		</div>

		<SectionHandoff from="alternatives" />
	</div>
</section>
